; NextDAAD TRANSPARENT TILEMAP PAPER + LAYER ORDER fixture
; (2026-08-18, rev 1).
;
; Compiled and staged by tests\build-tests.ps1 -TmOver, which also
; generates the full-frame card with tests\art\mktmover.py (001.NXI,
; 256x192) and copies build\nextdaad.nex in. sd\TMOVER\ is emptied and
; refilled on every run and holds nothing but GAME.DDB, 001.NXI and
; NEXTDAAD.NEX - copy the folder to the card and launch NEXTDAAD.NEX
; from inside it.
;
; The compiled bytes are asserted by the harness on every run, whether
; or not -TmOver is given: the GFX 0 17 / GFX 1 17 parameter pairs and
; the PAPER 227 / INK 227 / PAPER 11 operands are checked in the .DDB
; itself, because DRC silently rewrites some condacts and "the source
; says so" has shipped a vacuous fixture in this project twice.
;
; ------------------------------------------------------------------
; WHAT THIS FIXTURE PUTS ON SCREEN, AND WHY EACH PIECE IS THERE
; ------------------------------------------------------------------
; THE CARD, FIRST. PICTURE 1 + DISPLAY 0 loads 001.NXI, a 256x192
; full-frame card with NO transparent pixel anywhere in it. That is the
; whole premise: text over a picture used to need a hole cut in the
; artwork, and the feature under test puts the TEXT LAYER on top
; instead, so the picture it is read against must be one an artist
; would actually ship - complete, with nothing punched out of it.
;
; A 256x192 Layer 2 surface is inset 32 pixels on both axes from the
; tilemap origin, so the card covers tilemap ROWS 4..27 and COLUMNS
; 8..71 exactly (the derivation is in tests\art\mktmover.py's header).
; Rows 0..2 carry the status line and rows 28..31 the prompt: both are
; OUTSIDE the card, so they stay readable in either layer order and a
; run is never left with no way to read its own state.
;
; PICTURE IS A CONDITION, so a failed load aborts its entry and the
; entry after it says so on the status line, instead of the fixture
; silently testing nothing - which is exactly how the 2026-08-03 sfxdi
; run failed.
;
; ------------------------------------------------------------------
; THE FOUR BANDS - one screen, all four combinations
; ------------------------------------------------------------------
; Four 2-row by 32-column windows at column 20, on tilemap rows 9-10,
; 13-14, 17-18 and 21-22. Each is CLSed to its own paper, so the whole
; rectangle carries that paper and not just the cells a character
; landed on, and then labels itself:
;
;   BAND A  PAPER 227, INK 15. 227 is the ONE logical colour that
;           reaches the hardware transparent colour (TXT_TRANSP_COLOUR,
;           src\nextdaad.inc). With the text on top the picture shows
;           through the paper and only the white glyphs are solid. The
;           band sits across the card's blue/orange quadrant seam, so
;           what shows through changes colour halfway along it and
;           cannot be mistaken for a flat fill.
;   BAND B  PAPER 1, INK 15 - ordinary opaque paper. With the text on
;           top this is a solid dark blue block that HIDES the picture.
;           The card carries the word HIDDEN on a black plate drawn
;           wholly inside this band's rectangle, so a band that has
;           gone transparent by accident reads out its own failure.
;   BAND C  PAPER 1, INK 227 - transparent GLYPHS. The paper is opaque
;           and the letter shapes are cut out of it, so the picture
;           shows through the strokes. The card carries a vertical
;           rainbow under this band, so the letters come out
;           MULTI-COLOURED and "the glyphs are holes" cannot be
;           confused with "the glyphs are printed in some colour".
;   BAND D  PAPER 11, INK 0. Classic colour 11 is bright magenta, whose
;           RRRGGGBB happens to be the transparent colour - it used to
;           punch a hole. pal_colour now shifts it to L2_TRANSP_DODGE,
;           so this band MUST render bright magenta and MUST NOT be a
;           hole. It is the regression that pays for the whole reserved
;           value.
;
; THE REST OF THE TILEMAP IS PAPER 227. Window 0 is the whole 80x32
; grid, cleared to transparent paper before anything else is drawn, so
; with the text on top everything except the four bands and the two
; opaque margin strips shows the picture. Without that clear the boot
; paper (black) would cover the card and every band would be judged
; against a black screen.
;
; ------------------------------------------------------------------
; THE CHOREOGRAPHY - it runs itself, so a timed capture can read it
; ------------------------------------------------------------------
; There is no way to drive an emulator's keyboard from the harness -
; SendKeys does not reach CSpect's emulated keyboard, checked
; 2026-08-18 - so the six states run themselves, with PAUSE holds
; between them and the status line naming the state on screen. PAUSE 0
; is 256 frames in a V2 database (h_pause, src\overlay0.asm) - about
; 5.1 seconds - and DRC's duration scaling leaves 0 alone, so each held
; state lasts two of them, about ten seconds. States 1 to 3 run TWICE,
; because state 4 restarts the fixture and the replay follows; the whole
; run reaches the prompt in about two minutes.
;
;   STATE 1  GFX 0 17, picture on top. This is the default and the
;            classic arrangement. NO BAND MAY SHOW: the card has no
;            hole in it, so any band that is visible here is a layer
;            order that never took, or a transparent pixel in the card.
;   STATE 2  GFX 1 17, text on top. All four bands appear at once and
;            each is read against its own rule above.
;   STATE 3  PICTURE 1 + DISPLAY 0 while still flipped. THE ONE
;            REGRESSION THIS FIXTURE EXISTS FOR: l2_enable runs on
;            every picture blit and flip, and it used to write NR $15
;            whole, which would revert a flipped order on the next
;            picture operation. It now composes the field from
;            gfxLayerOrder, so state 3 must look exactly like state 2.
;   STATE 4  RESTART while flipped. THE RESET SITES ARE THE OTHER HALF
;            of this feature: the layer order is transient and returns
;            to picture-on-top at game start, at RESTART and at a
;            same-part LOAD/RAMLOAD, and the register follows
;            IMMEDIATELY at each of those because the reset sites call
;            the resident composer instead of waiting for the next
;            picture operation. "Immediately" is only observable if
;            nothing redraws in between, so this state flips the order,
;            goes to location 0 and RESTARTs; the AT 0 entry of process
;            0 then prints its caption and holds for two PAUSEs BEFORE
;            process 6 rebuilds the screen and process 7 blits the
;            card. During that hold nothing whatever has touched Layer
;            2 and the four bands are still in the tilemap, so they
;            must ALREADY be behind the picture. If they are still on
;            top and only sink when the card is redrawn, the reset site
;            is not calling gfx_layer_apply. It runs ONCE - fOnce
;            latches it, and RESTART does not touch flags - and the
;            choreography then replays states 1 to 3 before going on.
;            On the very FIRST boot the same hold shows a blank screen,
;            because nothing has been drawn yet; that is expected and
;            is not a result.
;   STATE 5  Text on top again, held, so a capture proves the screen
;            was genuinely flipped immediately before state 6.
;   STATE 6  RAMLOAD, the same-part reload path. RAMSAVE is taken at
;            the end of process 7, AFTER the move to location 1, so the
;            reload leaves the player where they are and the main loop
;            redraws NOTHING - no picture operation stands between the
;            reload and what is on the glass. The order must be
;            picture-on-top and the bands must be gone. After the hold
;            the prompt opens and the verbs below take over.
;
; The verbs below drive the same states by hand, and RECAL is the
; RAMLOAD leg on demand.
;
; ------------------------------------------------------------------
; THE VERBS
; ------------------------------------------------------------------
;   FLIP    GFX 1 17, text on top          FLOP  GFX 0 17, picture on top
;   DRAW    PICTURE 1 + DISPLAY 0, the state-3 regression on demand
;   STORE   RAMSAVE                        RECAL RAMLOAD 255
;   RESET   GOTO 0 + RESTART
;   LHIDE   EXTERN 0 9, hide Layer 2       LSHOW EXTERN 0 10, show it
;
; GFX 0 17 and GFX 1 17 are each authored EXACTLY ONCE, in processes 3
; and 2, and every other site calls that process. The harness asserts
; the compiled parameter pairs are present, and a guard nobody has seen
; fail is not evidence - authoring each pair once is what lets a
; deliberate break in the source make the assertion fire.
;
; LHIDE and LSHOW reach l2_disable / l2_enable through EXTERN vectors 9
; and 10, which exist in DEBUG builds only; in a Release build those
; vectors forward to a loaded XBN and this fixture stages none, so both
; verbs are silent no-ops there. They are the only way to reach "Layer
; 2 hidden" without an interpreter change, and the rule they test is
; that GFX 1 17 composes the priority field ALONE and must not re-show
; a deliberately hidden Layer 2.
;
; THE PAGER IS OFF EVERYWHERE. Every window is put in MODE 2 (h_mode
; writes WIN_FLAGS, bit 1 = disable More - src\overlay0.asm), because a
; More... prompt printed into a 2-row band would destroy the band it is
; measuring. No band message carries a newline either: each ends with a
; space, which is what flushes the word buffer (src\print.asm prn_char)
; without advancing a 2-row window's cursor far enough to scroll.
;
; NO SOUND, NO MUSIC, NO SECOND PICTURE. Nothing is staged but the
; card, so a symptom cannot be an audio interaction or a stale asset
; winning the probe chain. The probe chain matters: NX2 variants are
; tried FIRST, so a leftover 001.NX2 would route the blit through
; gfx_row_scatter320 and change the geometry under the test. sd\TMOVER\
; is emptied on every stage and holds three files.

; System flags used by name (DAAD BLANK template spellings)
#define fPlayer             38
; The one-shot latch that stops the automatic RESTART leg looping. It
; must survive RESTART, and it does: h_restart wipes the process stack
; and the draw-target block and touches no flag (src\overlay0.asm).
#define fOnce               100

/CTL
_
/VOC
FLIP    60  verb
FLOP    61  verb
DRAW    62  verb
STORE   63  verb
RECAL   64  verb
RESET   65  verb
LHIDE   66  verb
LSHOW   67  verb
/STX
/0  "It's too dark to see anything."
/1  "I can also see:"
/2  "What now?"
/3  "What next?"
/4  "What should I do now?"
/5  "What should I do next?"
/6  "I didn't understand."
/7  "I can't go in that direction."
/8  "I don't know that verb - try FLIP FLOP DRAW STORE RECAL RESET LHIDE LSHOW."
/9  "I have with me:"
/10 "I am wearing:"
/11 " "
/12 "Are you sure?"
/13 "Another go?"
/14 " "
/15 "OK."
/16 " "
/17 " "
/18 " "
/19 " "
/20 " "
/21 " "
/22 " "
/23 "I'm not wearing one of those."
/24 "I can't. My hands are full."
/25 "I already have the _."
/26 "There isn't one of those here."
/27 "I can't carry any more things."
/28 "I don't have one of those."
/29 "I'm already wearing the _."
/30 "Y"
/31 "N"
/32 "More..."
/33 ">"
/34 " "
/35 " "
/36 "I now have the _."
/37 "I'm now wearing the _."
/38 "I've removed the _."
/39 "I've dropped the _."
/40 "I can't wear the _."
/41 "I can't remove the _."
/42 "I can't remove the _. My hands are full."
/43 "The _ weighs too much for me."
/44 "The _ is in the"
/45 "The _ isn't in the"
/46 ", "
/47 " and "
/48 ".#n"
/49 "I don't have the _."
/50 "I'm not wearing the _."
/51 "."
/52 "There isn't one of those in the"
/53 "Nothing."
/54 " "
/55 " "
/56 " "
/57 "I/O Error"
/58 " "
/59 "File name error."
/60 "Type in name of file."
/MTX
/0  "NEXTDAAD TMOVER - TRANSPARENT PAPER AND LAYER ORDER#n"
/1  "VERBS  FLIP TEXT ON TOP   FLOP PICTURE ON TOP   DRAW REDRAW THE CARD#n       STORE RAMSAVE   RECAL RAMLOAD   RESET RESTART   LHIDE LSHOW LAYER 2#n"
/2  "PAPER 227 SEE THROUGH "
/3  "PAPER 1 OPAQUE BLOCK "
/4  "INK 227 STENCIL "
/5  "PAPER 11 MAGENTA "
/6  "STATE 1 OF 6 - PICTURE ON TOP - NO BAND MAY SHOW "
/7  "STATE 2 OF 6 - TEXT ON TOP - ALL FOUR BANDS SHOW "
/8  "STATE 3 OF 6 - REDRAWN WHILE FLIPPED - MUST LOOK LIKE STATE 2 "
/9  "ALL SIX STATES DONE - READY - TYPE A VERB "
/10 "PICTURE 1 FAILED - NO 001 NXI STAGED - NOTHING HERE IS A RESULT "
/11 "STATE 4 OF 6 OR BOOT - RESTARTED WHILE FLIPPED - NOTHING HAS TOUCHED LAYER 2 - THE PICTURE MUST ALREADY BE ON TOP "
/12 "RAMLOAD - ORDER MUST BE PICTURE ON TOP WITH NO PICTURE OPERATION "
/13 "STATE 5 OF 6 - TEXT ON TOP - RAMLOAD COMES NEXT AND NOTHING WILL REDRAW "
/14 "STATE 6 OF 6 - AFTER RAMLOAD - PICTURE ON TOP WITH NO PICTURE OPERATION "
/OTX
/0 "a brass lamp"
/LTX
/0 ""
/1 ""
/CON
/0
/1
/OBJ
/0    1   1   _ _  _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _   _ _
/PRO 0
; The main loop. Location 0 is the "not painted yet" state and is
; reached again only by a RESTART leg - state 4 of the choreography, or
; the RESET verb - so the AT 0 entry is both the boot path and the
; reset observation window. The caption and
; the two PAUSE holds run BEFORE process 6 rebuilds the screen and
; before process 7 touches Layer 2 at all, which is what makes "the
; order was restored immediately" an observable claim rather than an
; assertion about source code.
;
; On boot, window 5 is still the default full-screen window, so the CLS
; below clears the whole grid and the caption prints at the top left.
; After a RESET it is the 3-row status strip process 6 shaped, so the
; same CLS leaves the four bands exactly where they were.
> _ _   AT 0
        WINDOW 5
        CLS
        MESSAGE 11
        PAUSE 0
        PAUSE 0
        GOTO 1
        PROCESS 6
        PROCESS 7
        PROCESS 8
> _ _   WINDOW 6
        PROCESS 1
/PRO 1
; The parser loop, template-shaped and trimmed. Every verb entry ends
; in DONE and NOT in RESTART: the template's movement path ends with
; RESTART ("absolute jump to start process 0"), and RESTART is one of
; the sites that resets the layer order, so a verb that ended that way
; would undo the flip it had just made.
> _ _     WINDOW 6
          PARSE 0
          REDO
> FLIP  _ PROCESS 2
          SYSMESS 15
          DONE
> FLOP  _ PROCESS 3
          SYSMESS 15
          DONE
> DRAW  _ PICTURE 1
          DISPLAY 0
          SYSMESS 15
          DONE
> STORE _ RAMSAVE
          SYSMESS 15
          DONE
> RECAL _ WINDOW 5
          CLS
          MESSAGE 0
          MESSAGE 12
          RAMLOAD 255
> RESET _ GOTO 0
          RESTART
> LHIDE _ EXTERN 0 9
          SYSMESS 15
          DONE
> LSHOW _ EXTERN 0 10
          SYSMESS 15
          DONE
> _ _     NEWTEXT
          SYSMESS 8
          REDO
/PRO 2
; TEXT ON TOP. The ONLY GFX 1 17 in this fixture - the choreography and
; the FLIP verb both come here, so the compiled byte run the harness
; asserts exists exactly once and a deliberate break in it cannot be
; masked by a second copy.
> _ _   GFX 1 17
/PRO 3
; PICTURE ON TOP. The only GFX 0 17, for the same reason. This is the
; interpreter's default, so the choreography issues it explicitly at
; state 1 rather than assuming it: a fixture that never asks for the
; default cannot tell "the default is correct" from "sub 17 ignored the
; request".
> _ _   GFX 0 17
/PRO 6
; Window layout, and nothing else. Window 0 is the whole grid cleared
; to TRANSPARENT paper - do this first, before the opaque strips, or
; they get cleared away again. Windows 5 and 6 are the two margin
; strips, outside the card's footprint, opaque so they stay readable in
; both layer orders. Windows 1 to 4 are the four bands; their colours
; and contents belong to process 7, so that a repaint can be reasoned
; about separately from the layout.
> _ _   WINDOW 0
        WINAT 0 0
        WINSIZE 32 80
        MODE 2
        PAPER 227
        INK 15
        CLS
        BORDER 0
> _ _   WINDOW 5
        WINAT 0 0
        WINSIZE 3 80
        MODE 2
        PAPER 0
        INK 15
        CLS
        MESSAGE 0
> _ _   WINDOW 6
        WINAT 28 0
        WINSIZE 4 80
        MODE 2
        PAPER 0
        INK 15
        CLS
        MESSAGE 1
> _ _   WINDOW 1
        WINAT 9 20
        WINSIZE 2 32
        MODE 2
> _ _   WINDOW 2
        WINAT 13 20
        WINSIZE 2 32
        MODE 2
> _ _   WINDOW 3
        WINAT 17 20
        WINSIZE 2 32
        MODE 2
> _ _   WINDOW 4
        WINAT 21 20
        WINSIZE 2 32
        MODE 2
; The pointer is shown here and never touched again. It is a hardware
; sprite and sprites stay above both layers in either order, so it must
; be visible in state 1 and state 2 alike - if it disappears when the
; order flips, the composer is writing more of NR $15 than the three
; priority bits.
> _ _   MOUSE 0 1
/PRO 7
; The card and the four bands. The card goes up FIRST so that a failed
; load is reported before any band is drawn and the run is stopped on
; the status line rather than being read as a transparency result.
> _ _   PICTURE 1
        DISPLAY 0
        SKIP 1
> _ _   WINDOW 5
        CLS
        MESSAGE 0
        MESSAGE 10
; Each band is CLSed to its own paper so the whole 2x32 rectangle
; carries it, then labelled. No message here ends in a newline - see
; the header.
> _ _   WINDOW 1
        PAPER 227
        INK 15
        CLS
        MESSAGE 2
> _ _   WINDOW 2
        PAPER 1
        INK 15
        CLS
        MESSAGE 3
> _ _   WINDOW 3
        PAPER 1
        INK 227
        CLS
        MESSAGE 4
> _ _   WINDOW 4
        PAPER 11
        INK 0
        CLS
        MESSAGE 5
; RAMSAVE last, and AFTER process 0 moved the player to location 1, so
; RECAL restores a position that redraws nothing - see the header.
> _ _   RAMSAVE
/PRO 8
; The timed choreography. Each entry names its state on the status
; line, makes the change, and holds for two PAUSE 0s so a timed capture
; lands inside it.
> _ _   WINDOW 5
        CLS
        MESSAGE 0
        MESSAGE 6
        PROCESS 3
        PAUSE 0
        PAUSE 0
> _ _   WINDOW 5
        CLS
        MESSAGE 0
        MESSAGE 7
        PROCESS 2
        PAUSE 0
        PAUSE 0
; State 3. PICTURE is a condition again: if it fails the entry aborts
; and the states below still run, so a missing card cannot leave the
; fixture wedged with no prompt.
> _ _   WINDOW 5
        CLS
        MESSAGE 0
        MESSAGE 8
        PICTURE 1
        DISPLAY 0
        PAUSE 0
        PAUSE 0
; State 4. RESTART while flipped, and it drives itself because nothing
; can type into an emulator: fOnce latches so the leg runs ONCE, and
; RESTART does not touch flags, so the latch survives the very thing it
; is guarding. The caption for this state is printed by the AT 0 entry
; of process 0, on the other side of the RESTART, because that is where
; the observation actually happens.
> _ _   ZERO fOnce
        SET fOnce
        PROCESS 2
        GOTO 0
        RESTART
; State 5. Flip again and HOLD, so a capture can prove the screen was
; genuinely flipped immediately before the reload - without this state
; the next one would be reporting on a screen nobody saw flip.
> _ _   WINDOW 5
        CLS
        MESSAGE 0
        MESSAGE 13
        PROCESS 2
        PAUSE 0
        PAUSE 0
; State 6. The player is back at location 1, so the main loop redraws
; nothing at all - no picture operation stands between the reload and
; what is on the glass, and the two holds are the observation window.
; RAMLOAD sets the done stamp (it continues into eng_set_done) but is
; NOT a terminator, so the holds after it do run - checked on the glass,
; not assumed.
> _ _   WINDOW 5
        CLS
        MESSAGE 0
        MESSAGE 14
        RAMLOAD 255
        PAUSE 0
        PAUSE 0
> _ _   WINDOW 5
        CLS
        MESSAGE 0
        MESSAGE 9
/END
